    <h1 class="rv in" style="font-size:clamp(2.2rem,5vw,4rem);max-width:20ch">Should You Take a Boat Trip From Funchal or From Calheta?</h1>
    <div class="artmeta rv in d1"><span>24 November 2026</span><span>5 min read</span><span>Funchal · Madeira</span></div>
  </div>
</header>

<section class="pad">
  <div class="wrap">
    <article class="article rv">
      <p class="lede">Choose the marina nearest to where you stay, unless one stretch of coast holds something you really want to see. From Funchal, boats run west past Câmara de Lobos, Cabo Girão and Fajã dos Padres; from Calheta, about 32 km further west by road, they show the quieter southwest of the island.</p>

      <h2>Which part of the coast does each marina show?</h2><p>The two marinas sit on the same south coast, but a short trip from each one covers a different stretch. That is the real difference, more than the boats.</p><h3>From Funchal</h3><p>A trip west from Marina do Funchal passes some of the most photographed shore on the island: the fishing boats in the bay at Câmara de Lobos, Cabo Girão rising 580 metres straight from the water, and Fajã dos Padres, farmland under the cliff with no road, only a cable car and the sea. Then come Ribeira Brava and, on a longer trip, Ponta do Sol. Chifbay's Day Trip follows exactly this line. You leave from, and come back to, the city itself, with Funchal climbing the hills behind you.</p><h3>From Calheta</h3><p>Calheta's marina sits next to a man-made beach of golden sand, opened in 2004 with sand brought mainly from Morocco and protected by breakwaters. To the west, the coast runs past villages squeezed under steep cliffs, such as Jardim do Mar and Paul do Mar, towards Ponta do Pargo and its lighthouse at the western end of the island. Many of the trips that leave Calheta are whale and dolphin watching trips. As everywhere on this island, no honest operator can promise a sighting.</p><h2>Where are you staying?</h2><p>This settles it for most people. The drive between the two towns is about 32 km on the expressway, roughly 30 to 45 minutes depending on traffic, and the same again to get back.</p><table><thead><tr><th>You are staying in</th><th>Nearest marina</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Funchal, Caniço or Câmara de Lobos</td><td>Funchal</td><td>A walk or a short taxi to the pontoon</td></tr><tr><td>Calheta, Jardim do Mar, Paul do Mar or Ponta do Pargo</td><td>Calheta</td><td>You save a long drive, twice</td></tr><tr><td>Ribeira Brava or Ponta do Sol</td><td>Either</td><td>Choose by the coast you want to see</td></tr></tbody></table><p>If you arrive on a cruise ship, the choice is made for you. The ships come into the port of Funchal, a short walk from the marina, and Calheta is too far for a port day.</p><p>A boat trip of two or three hours should not need an hour and a half of driving around it. If you are based in Funchal, the coast west of the city fills a short trip on its own. If you are still choosing a base, our guide on <a href="/posts/where-to-stay-in-madeira">where to stay in Madeira</a> compares the towns.</p><h2>Is the sea different on each side?</h2><p>Both marinas are on the south side of the island, which is sheltered compared with the north. That is why boat trips run from both for most of the year. On a given day one stretch can be calmer than the other, and no rule says which. The forecast decides, and on our trips, so does the skipper.</p><p>What really changes is the scenery above you. West of Funchal you pass under some of the highest sea cliffs on the island, which makes even a calm day feel big. West of Calheta the villages are smaller and the shore emptier. If you want to know why this side of Madeira is gentler, read <a href="/posts/why-is-madeira-south-coast-calmer-for-boats">why the south coast is calmer for boats</a>.</p><h2>Which one has the better light?</h2><p>Both face south and both see the sun go down to the west, so the difference is what the light falls on.</p><ul><li><strong>From Funchal</strong>, an evening trip heads west along the cliffs with the low sun ahead, and on the way back the city's lights come on across the hills of the amphitheatre. That is the route of our <a href="/sunset-cruise">Sunset Trip</a>, which heads home from Cabo Girão or from Ribeira Brava, depending on its length.</li><li><strong>From Calheta</strong>, the evening is darker and quieter, with far fewer lights on shore and a more natural feel.</li></ul><p>If the sunset itself is the point, the west has the classic land viewpoint at Ponta do Pargo, which belongs to the municipality of Calheta. You can stand at the lighthouse in the evening and never set foot on a boat.</p><h2>Can you see both coasts in one holiday?</h2><p>Yes, and it is a good use of a week. Take the boat from Funchal one day, so you see Cabo Girão and Fajã dos Padres from the water. On another day, drive west on the expressway: an afternoon on the sand at Calheta, a slow road through Jardim do Mar or Paul do Mar, and the evening at the lighthouse of Ponta do Pargo. You get the southwest from land without a second boat trip, and the two days feel completely different.</p><h2>So which should you choose?</h2><h3>Choose Funchal if</h3><ul><li>you stay in or near Funchal, or you arrive on a cruise ship;</li><li>you want Cabo Girão and Fajã dos Padres from the sea;</li><li>you want to swim and paddle board under the cliffs, then walk to lunch in town.</li></ul><h3>Choose Calheta if</h3><ul><li>you stay in the west and the drive to Funchal would eat the morning;</li><li>your main wish is a whale and dolphin trip, the focus of many Calheta boats;</li><li>you want a day on a sand beach with a boat trip as one part of it.</li></ul><p>To be clear about our side of it: Chifbay only leaves from Marina do Funchal. If you stay in Calheta and would rather not drive, a boat from Calheta is the sensible choice.</p><h2>What does a Funchal trip look like in practice?</h2><p>Our Day Trip goes out at 10:00 or 14:00, for 2h30 or 3h. On the way you pass under <a href="/cabo-girao-boat-tour">Cabo Girão by boat</a> and stop at <a href="/faja-dos-padres-boat-trip">Fajã dos Padres</a> to swim and paddle, with a second swim at Ribeira Brava. Pick the 3h version and the boat reaches Ponta do Sol, the nearest a trip from Funchal comes to Calheta. The whole boat is yours, for up to 5 guests, as on every <a href="/private-boat-tour-madeira">private boat tour in Madeira</a> we run.</p><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><h3>How far is Calheta from Funchal?</h3><p>About 32 km by road, roughly 30 to 45 minutes on the expressway depending on traffic.</p><h3>Is Calheta beach natural?</h3><p>No. It is a man-made beach of golden sand, opened in 2004 with sand brought mainly from Morocco and protected by breakwaters.</p><h3>Does Chifbay run trips from Calheta?</h3><p>No. Every trip starts at Marina do Funchal, right on the city's seafront. The skipper meets you on the pontoon.</p><h3>Which marina is better for seeing dolphins?</h3><p>Many Calheta trips focus on whales and dolphins. Dolphins can appear anywhere along the south coast, but no operator can guarantee a sighting.</p>

      <blockquote>Same island, same sun, two coasts: let your hotel&#x27;s address make the first cut.</blockquote>
    </article>
    <p class="pref-src" data-pref-source><span>Enjoying our Madeira guides?</span> <a href="https://www.google.com/preferences/source?q=chifbay.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="18" height="18" aria-hidden="true"><path fill="#4285F4" d="M23.5 12.3c0-.8-.1-1.6-.2-2.3H12v4.5h6.5a5.6 5.6 0 0 1-2.4 3.6v3h3.9c2.2-2.1 3.5-5.1 3.5-8.8z"/><path fill="#34A853" d="M12 24c3.2 0 6-1.1 7.9-2.9l-3.9-3c-1.1.7-2.4 1.2-4 1.2-3.1 0-5.7-2.1-6.6-4.9h-4v3.1A12 12 0 0 0 12 24z"/><path fill="#FBBC05" d="M5.4 14.4a7.2 7.2 0 0 1 0-4.7V6.6h-4a12 12 0 0 0 0 10.8l4-3z"/><path fill="#EA4335" d="M12 4.8c1.7 0 3.3.6 4.6 1.8l3.4-3.4A12 12 0 0 0 1.4 6.6l4 3.1C6.3 6.9 8.9 4.8 12 4.8z"/></svg>Add Chifbay as a preferred source on Google</a></p>

